## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of OS file locking, race conditions, deadlock avoidance, and IO visibility in a beginner-friendly context.

## Key Concepts
- Advisory locks with fcntl
- F_SETLKW vs F_SETLK
- Deadlock prevention by fixed lock order
- fsync for visibility

## Code Example
```c
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>

static int lock_log(int fd) {
  struct flock fl; memset(&fl, 0, sizeof(fl)); fl.l_type = F_WRLCK; fl.l_whence = SEEK_SET; fl.l_start = 0; fl.l_len = 0; return fcntl(fd, F_SETLKW, &fl);
}
static int unlock_log(int fd) {
  struct flock fl; memset(&fl, 0, sizeof(fl)); fl.l_type = F_UNLCK; fl.l_whence = SEEK_SET; fl.l_start = 0; fl.l_len = 0; return fcntl(fd, F_SETLK, &fl);
}

void safe_log_write(int fd, const char *msg) {
  if (lock_log(fd) == -1) return;
  write(fd, msg, strlen(msg));
  fsync(fd);
  unlock_log(fd);
}
```

